<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/expert/philosophy-of-religion">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy of religion examines <strong>the rational arguments for and against theism</strong>
    — staged charitably, at full strength. It is philosophy of religion's claim to seriousness:
    the arguments have canonical forms, named objections, and counter-replies, argued for a
    millennium. This lesson presents the canonical forms and their strongest critics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four classical arguments and their strongest critics" widget="equationStep" config={philrelCfg} />

  <h2>The debates and their referees</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hume on miracles:</strong> which is more likely — that the report is false, or that nature was suspended? The evidential asymmetry argument still structures every miracle discussion.</li>
    <li><strong>Religious experience:</strong> mystics report union with the ultimate across cultures (James's Varieties, 1902). Do consistent reports validate the object? The "principle of credulity" (Swinburne) vs the neurotheology rebuttal.</li>
    <li><strong>Faith and reason:</strong> fideism (faith above reason), evidentialism (belief needs evidence — Clifford's "wrong always... wherever believed upon insufficient evidence"), and Reformed epistemology (belief in God can be "properly basic"). The map of the current argument.</li>
    <li><strong>The honest outcome:</strong> no argument converts on its own; each survives in refined form. The discipline's value is clarity about exactly what is claimed, what follows, and what remains open.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Anselm, Proslogion (1078); Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779); Plantinga, God, Freedom, and Evil (1974); Mackie, The Miracle of Theism (1982).</p>
</TopicPage>
